  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nKDDC radar observations late Saturday evening show the \ndecaying remnants of an elevated mesoscale convective system \nmoving northeast across our northern zones. This activity will \npose little threat other than heavy rain and frequent lightning \nas it continues northeast. Otherwise, northerly winds in the \nwake of an earlier cold front passage will bring much cooler air\nequatorward overnight, with lows dropping into the upper 50s \nnorthwest to the upper 60s southeast.\n\nDaytime today, guidance indicates an upper level shortwave \ntrough currently over the northern Rockies, as per latest water\nvapor satellite imagery, will inch eastward onto the northern \nplains. At the surface, northeasterly winds will persist across \nsouthwest KS as high pressure settles into the area, supporting \na long-awaited break from the record-breaking heat as afternoon \nhighs will range from the low/mid 70s near I-70 to the low/mid \n80s along the KS/OK border. By late afternoon/early evening, \nHREF members suggest thunderstorm development is possible in the\npost-frontal upslope flow regime over east-central CO and west-\ncentral KS, moving southeast through the overnight period over \nour western zones. Roughly 1000-1500 J/kg and 30-35 kts of deep-\nlayer shear will be in place, supporting a severe risk with any \nconvection that forms, with large hail and damaging wind gusts \nthe primary threats.\n\nShort range ensembles maintain upper level westerly flow across \nthe central plains daytime Monday between a pair of shortwave \ntroughs over the northern plains/upper Midwest and the central \nCA coast. As a result, southwest KS will remain under the \ninfluence of weak surface high pressure and light northeasterly \nwinds, both of which will help reinforce the cool airmass over \nour area and promote afternoon highs in the mid 70s to low 80s. \nThunderstorm activity will also likely be muted given the lack \nof upper level forcing.\n\nTuesday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles \nagree weak upper level ridging over the southern plains and a \nconvoluted pattern elsewhere will engender a continuation of the\ncool, seasonable temperatures across the central plains with \nafternoon highs in the mid 70s to mid 80s each day through at \nleast the end of the business week. Precipitation chances will \nalso exist each day as the NBM has at least some chance pops \n(25-54%) every day into the weekend, with the highest \nprobability surrounding Wednesday and Thursday.",
